nmet medical need with regards to  treating duchenne's muscular dystrophy  so duchenne's is a genetic disease that  affects caucasian males has a high  frequency of about one in three thousand  five hundred there's no cure for this  disease and everybody who has this  disease dies in their 40s or 30s  duchenne's muscular dystrophy is caused  by a mutation in the dystrophin gene  dystrophin is shown on this slide here  and it is needed for maintaining muscle  health and the reason you need  dystrophin in muscle tissue is because  our muscle cells are constantly getting  stretched because of the of their  contraction cycles and that stretching  uh imposes a lot of physical damage on  them and dystrophin is basically there  to protect them from the stretching  cycles the stretching and contraction  cycles that muscle tissue undergoes  repeatedly  so in people who suffer from duchenes  they don't make dystrophin and their  muscle tissue starts to die off and that  eventually leads to death  now now uh crispr-based therapeutics  have the potential to treat duchenne's  muscular dystrophy by by cutting the  dystrophin gene at the site of its  mutation and correcting it via homology  directed dna repair so it has tremendous  potential for treating duchene's  muscular dystrophy  so we investigated  if crispr gold could rescue mice  suffering from duchene's muscular  dystrophy via the triggering of homology  directed dna repair so what we did is we  made crispr gold that had a cast 9  that was designed to cut the dystrophin  mutation and also a donor dna that was  designed to correct it this was done in  collaboration with irena convoy's lab  and then for these experiments we used  the mdx mouse model the mdx mouse model  is a mouse that has a mutation in the  dystrophin gene and exon 21. it's a c to  t mutation that generates a stop codon  and so mdx mice do not make dystrophin  so we injected these mice in their  muscle tissue with with crispr gold  designed to correct this mutation by hdr  and after two weeks we we sacrificed  these mice and sequenced them to see if  we were correcting the dystrophin  mutation the results are shown here  the y-axis is the hdr efficiency  measured by deep sequencing the x-axis  is the two groups what you can see is  that if you inject these mice in their  muscle tissue with crispr gold  we're getting up to about a 5.4  correction efficiency so 5.4 of the  genomic dna isolated in the muscle  tissue near the vicinity of the  injection had been corrected back to the  wild type sequence and in the control it  was around zero  5.4 is a fairly high it's a fairly is a  reasonable editing rate uh that's that's  speculated to be where you would start  seeing clinical benefits in the case of  duchene's  we also analyze these mice for uh  for the expression of dystrophin protein  all the way on the right here is a  histology slide of a wild type mouse  that has been stained with a dystrophin  antibody you can see that there's a lot  of disrobin dystrophin staining all the  way on the left is the control mdx mouse  uh if you stand with the dystrophin  antibody there is no standing as you  would expect they don't make dystrophin  however in the crease in the case of mdx  mice treated with crispr gold you can  see that there are regions where you  have very high dystrophin positivity and  so it can be as high as 60 percent  demonstrating that we are uh inducing  the re-expression of the dystrophin  protein  uh based on these studies uh the lead  author of this work his name was kunwu  lee  he was a graduate student in my lab he  formed a company called gen edit  which is now focused on developing  crispr gold and other cas9 delivery  vehicles and ultimately wants to bring  these type of technologies into clinical  trials uh what i'll talk about on the  next few slides is a collaboration  between our lab and genetic and another  lab uh at the university of texas and  san antonio in particular professor hey  young lee's lab  there are new there are numerous organs  where gene editing can have therapeutic  effects  of these the brain is perhaps one of the  most interesting  and this is because neurological  diseases such as huntington's and  alzheimer's affect millions of people  each year their incidence is  dramatically increasing because of the  aging population  and it turns out that it's actually  quite challenging to develop  therapeutics for brain diseases and this  is for many reasons one of them being  the blood-brain barrier and the other  one is the toxic effects of globally  inhibiting neuronal signaling pathways  we think that gene editing accomplished  by a local intracranial injection has  tremendous potential for treating brain  diseases and this is because if you do a  local intracranial injection you will  overcome the problems with the  blood-brain barrier  uh you will also just get local editing  and that should uh mitigate some of the  toxic side effects of brain therapeutics  and finally because gene editing is  permanent you'll only have to do this  once and doing intracranial injections  is already routinely done in a variety  of clinical scenarios such as for  treating epilepsy however despite its  potential very little is known about  brain gene editing  so to learn something about brain gene  editing we started a collaboration with  with gen edit and professor hey young  lee's lab at the university of texas in  san antonio  and we shipped them crispr gold that was  designed to do gene editing in the brain  and they investigated it in a mouse  model called the ai9 mouse model so the  ai9 mouse model is shown here it's  composed of a mouse that has the td  tomato gene  upstream of a stop codon integrated in  every one of its cells so these mice do  not make the td tomato gene which is the  same thing as the red fluorescent  protein  because of the stop codon however if you  cut out the stop signal by a crisper you  can then get the re-expression of the td  tomato gene which you can then easily  measure via fluorescence  so we made crispr gold that was designed  to cut out the stop codon  and shipped it to hey young's lab she  injected it into the striatum and  hippocampus and we've made two flavors  of crispr gold for this experiment one  that had cas9 and the other one that had  the gene editing enzyme cpf1  so the results of this experiment are  shown here which you can see on the top  section is those are the cas9 injected  mice  below that is the cpf1 injected mice so  the mice that were injected with cas9  have a lot of little red dots each one  of those red dots in the yellow square  is a cell that got two cuts  and uh had the re-expression of the td  tomato gene and so in both cases uh cas9  or cpf1 the amount of red dots is much  higher than in the control which really  has no red dots and  this demonstrates that we are able to  see some editing uh after an  intracranial injection of cas9 or cpf1  complex to crispr gold and that it's  actually reasonably efficient we were  able to get a number on this and it's  around 10 to 15 percent it's worth  mentioning that this is a fairly  challenging edit to do because you need  to make two cuts and then the gene has  to recombine cast iron is really good at  cutting but it's not particularly good  at doing recombination  so we therefore investigated if crispr  gold could do editing in neurons  specifically and to investigate this we  used um a mouse model where yfp or the  yellow fluorescent protein is only  expressed in neurons and we made crispr  gold that was designed to knock it out  by nhej  so we made crispr gold that was designed  to knock out the yfp gene we shipped it  to hey young's lab she injects it into  the hippocampus and then counts four yfp  negative cells the results are shown on  the slide here and again we made two  flavors one that has cas9 and the other  one that has cpf1  the y-axis here is the percent of yfp  cells on a histology slide the x-axis  is  the various groups and what you can see  is that  in the case of control  uh control mice about 30 of the cells or  neurons on a histology slide that  dramatically decreases all the way down  to about 10 percent uh with crispr gold  that either has cast iron or cpf1 uh  demonstrating that cas9 and or cpf1  complex to crispr gold can fairly  efficiently knock out genes uh after an  intracranial injection we're getting  knockout rates of around 50 percent  based on this we investigated if we  could um  if we could if crispr gold could  generate therapeutic effects in the  brain uh by by intracranial gene editing  and we selected fragile x-related autism  as an initial disease target  we selected this these this disease  because of the high unmet medical need  fragile x-related autism is the most  common hereditary form of autism it's  caused by a triplet repeat expansion in  the fmr1 gene that has the effect of  knocking out the fm1 gene  the fm01 gene is a is a global inhibitor  of protein expression and it turns out  that if you knock it out by this triple  triplet repeat expansion  you get a variety of pathologies in  terms of learning and development  it's also known that  the m glue r5  it's also known that the mglu r5  signaling pathway also is is  is over activated uh in patients who  suffer from fragile x-related autism  and so we investigated if we could treat  fragile x-related autism by knocking out  mglu r5  in um in a  mouse model for fragile x-ray related  autism which is composed of a fmr1  knockout mouse  so we made crispr gold that was designed  to knock out the m glue r5 gene  sent it to hay young's lab and she  injected it into fmr1 knockout mice or  wild-type mice and then looked for the  knockdown of mlura5 via  immunohistochemistry on histology slides  so the results are shown here  the y-axis is the number of m-glu r5  positive cells and the x-axis is the  various groups  so you can see that in both control  uh  in both wild type and fm1 knockout mice  the number of mglu r5 positive cells  dramatically decreases uh after  treatment with crispr gold so in the  case of wild type in the case of the  wild type mice it goes from 60  down to about 35 percent and the you see  similar numbers in the fm1 knockout mice  so this demonstrates that we can fairly  efficiently knock out mglu r5 after an  intracranial injection of crispr gold  so based on this we did a variety of  behavioral studies to see if uh crispr  gold could protect mice or rescue mice  from the repetitive behaviors that are  observed with autism and it turns out  that the effermon knockout mice uh  exhibits many of these obsessive  phenotypes one of them is that they jump  up and down obsessively in a cage and so  we investigated if and  and so not only do they jump up and down  obsessively in a cage it's also known  that this phenotype is caused by the  striatum  and so we investigated if knocking out  mlr5 in the striatum can rescue mice  from this phenotype  the results of this experiment are shown  here the y-axis is the number of jumps  in a cage the x-axis is the various  groups so you can see that f number one  knockout mice uh jump up and down much  more than the control mice so fmr1  knockout must jump up and down about 20  times in this experiment  whereas the control mice there's almost  no jump so there's a dramatic increase  in the jumping behavior of these mice  and that jumping behavior is decreased  um more than  more than about fourfold by partially  knocking out m glue r5 in the striatum  that's the m glue r5 crisper  bar  versus the control bar the black versus  the gray  there we also investigated some other  phenotypes such as line crossing which  have nothing to do nothing to do with  the stridom  line crossing is a phenotype that comes  from the hippocampus and in that case we  didn't actually see any effect as we  would expect given the fact that our  editing is only in the stride  all right i'm now going to talk about  another uh cas9 delivery strategy uh  that's focused on making cas9 protein  conjugates everything i've talked about  up to now is based upon making  have all been nanoparticle-based  delivery vehicles for cast nine  uh however nanoparticles have certain  limitations in particular they're big  crispr gold in particular is around 200  nanometers uh the large size of crispr  gold means that there'll be a lot of  tissues that it will not be able to  access  we're therefore very interested in being  able to make cas9  cas9 conjugates with polymers or  peptides that can do the delivery  by itself because that should be much  much smaller however  conjugating things to cast nine is  problematic because if you modify the  lysine residues of cas9 you will almost  certainly inactivate it to get around  this problem we developed a new linker  called the dec linker  which is a disulfide self-immolative  linker shown on this slide here the dec  linker is a combination of two linkers  shown on the top here where where it  says vector  dec protein and the dec linker is  composed of two uh linkers a thio ethyl  carbonate linker combined with the 1 6  elimination linker  which is then modifies which which then  modifies the lysine residue of your  protein  so this allows you to conjugate things  to proteins however in the presence of  glutathione or thiols present in the  cell  what ends up happening is the disulfide  bond is broken and that triggers two  cascade reactions two self-immolative  reactions which end up  completely releasing the protein without  any any tag or any modification of the  lysine residues  the dec linker has numerous applications  for protein delivery uh we've used it  for three different applications i'll  talk about two of them today  the first one that we'll talk about is  is  using it to enhance the diffusion of  proteins in brain tissue and this is  accomplished by a protein percolation so  this so using the dec linker we've been  able to conjugate peg to cast nine and  uh maintain the activity of cas9 and  also improve its diffusion through brain  tissue  in addition we've been able to conjugate  self-penetrating peptides to cast iron  and get self-delivering cast ions that  are also enzymatically active  and finally uh an application i won't  talk about is that we've also been able  to conjugate donor dna to to cas9 and  can improve the the ratio the rate of  hdr uh using again the dec linker  so so now i'm going to talk about  using the dec linker to conjugate peg to  cast 9. so we made a reagent that's  composed of peg conjugated to the dec  linker with a nitrophenyl group on there  and that allows us to stick peg onto  proteins in one step  there's a lot of interest in being able  to conjugate peg to proteins because it  reduces immunogenicity  and can increase circulation half-lives  and can also  improve tissue diffusion  through mucus and also brain tissue  however if you just conjugate peg  randomly to the lysine residues of a  protein you will almost certainly  inactivate it and hence the rationale  for the dec linker so the scheme shown  here explains how we're doing this so we  have the native cas9 with all its lysine  residues cast 9 has many lysine residues  we then mix it with the peg dec linker  which is called peg disulfide linker  that  sticks the peg groups randomly all over  the cast 9  and that basically generates a cas9 peg  conjugate that's going to be inactive  however once the cast ion goes inside of  the cell and there's glutathione uh the  the um  the the peg comes off and it comes off  completely and there's no residual  modification of lysine residue so you  get back your original casing which  should then be enzymatically active  so we started a collaboration with ross  wilson's laboratory at uc berkeley and  also chris benkowitz's lab at  the university of ohio  and  we investigated if  conjugating peg to cas9 can improve its  diffusion through tissue  so we shipped cas9 peg  conjugated through the dec linker to the  bankwitz lab  and they injected it into the stridom of  mice using a technique called convection  enhanced delivery and this is a  procedure where you can inject up to  seven microliters into them  into the striatum of a mouse and then uh  immediately after doing the injection uh  they sacrificed the mice and looked for  the presence of the cas9 via  immunohistochemistry so they stained  with an antibody the control for this  experiment was just an injection of the  same amount of cas9  so you can see on the right hemisphere  is what the immunohistochemistry of the  mouse looks like after injection with  cast 9 dec peg and the left is  uh what it looks like after injection  with cast nine so you can see and the  brown here is where the cast iron is so  you can see that in the in the in the  hemisphere that was injected with cas9  peg  uh there's brown staining all throughout  the striatum and that's suggesting that  the cast 9 is really diffusing quite  freely through the brain tissue given  that this is a  specific staining with an antibody and  that is  in stark contrast to what it looks like  uh after you um if you just inject the  native cas9  that seems to get stuck in the brain  tissue not surprising the the brain  tissue is very sticky so this  demonstrates that we can improve the  tissue diffusion of casting by  conjugating peg to it via the dec linker  we've also been able to conjugate cell  penetrating peptides to cast 9  using the dec linker to do this we made  this compound shown here  uh which is com which which we're  calling cppdc and that's composed of the  cell penetrating peptide  nine arginine nine so nine arginines and  it's now conjugated to a fluorescent dye  so we can easily  we can easily track it and then it also  has been it also has another functional  handle on there which is this  nitrophenyl group conjugated to the  arginine via a disulfide linkage so the  nitrophenyl group here is what actually  does the conjugation to the protein  it'll react with the lysine residues on  the protein and then once in the  presence of thiols everything comes off  and you get the the traceless release of  the protein so that's what's shown on  the bottom here  the way we do the chemistry is we take  the cas9 uh  which is which is shown on the left  native cast 9 we mix it with cpp dec  that randomly conjugates to all the  lysine residues on the protein again  generating what's most likely an  inactive protein but then once it  reaches the cytoplasm all of the cpps  come off because of the disulfide  reduction and you get the unmodified  protein which should be enzymatically  active  so we actually investigated if  this is actually so so what we did was  we made cas9 cpp by doing the chemistry  i just showed you uh where we had about  40 cpps per cast 9 and then we  investigated its activity before and  after reduction with glutathione and  this is five millimolar glutathione  so the on the left you can see the gel  uh where we investigated the enzymatic  activity of cast iron cpp where it was  mixed with the guide rna uh  where it was mixed with the donor where  it was mixed with template dna uh that  was specific for the guide rna and on  the right of that is um  is the  the quantitation of this gel so the  y-axis is nuclease activity and the  x-axis is the various groups and what  you can see here is that cas9 cpp so if  you have the cpps conjugated to the cas9  it is actually almost completely  inactive not surprising given the that  there are a lot of lysine residues on  cas9 that are essential however if you  now reduce the the cpp off you can  almost get a hundred percent activity  back and there's no difference between  the cas9 cpp plus gsh and the wild type  cas9 demonstrating that the linker  really is traceless and it's it's not  leaving any tag on there and that we can  recover the protein activity  finally we did investigate if cas9 cpp  could edit  cells  so in this experiment we took cas9 cpp  that had a guide rna that targets the  gfp gene and we incubated it with  hex cells that express gfp  and uh  and then after um 48 hours we isolated  these cells and sent them for sanger  sequencing so the results are shown here  the y-axis is the editing efficiency and  the x-axis is the various groups so the  the green bar is a control where if you  just take cas9 rnp and add it to cells  you only see about 0.3 percent of  editing is determined by sanger  sequencing again almost the background  levels and that increases all the way up  to 5.1 percent if you conjugate uh  arginine 9 as a cpp to the cast 9  via the dec linker so we saw a big  enhancement in the amount of editing uh  more than a 10-fold almost a 20-fold  increase in editing over the wild-type  cast 9 and at least in this experiment  this is comparable this is comparable to  life effectively so we're now in the  process of of optimizing this and and  finding other  ways of improving the delivery  efficiency of uh cas9 conjugates  so in summary i've talked about two  different methodologies for delivering  the cas9 the first one was was a  technology called crispr gold which was  uh able to do gene editing in muscle  tissue and also in the brain and finally  um the second technology i talked about  was the dec linker which allowed us to  make uh which allowed us to conjugate uh  peptides and polymers to cas9 and  in a reversible manner and also retain  the activity of the cas9  so i want to thank the people that did  the work um the  all the crispr gold work was done by one  very talented graduate student named  kunwuli who's now the ceo of genetic  and uh the dec linker was done by a  postdoc named mao mao he so finally  thank you thank you for your attendance
 

